Buddhism: completing the core reading (@buddhist-core-completion: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @buddhism, #philosophy @buddhist-further-reading, #buddhism @buddhist-further-reading, #philosophy @buddhist-textual-study, #buddhism @buddhist-textual-study) { r_562HHAD8ARXMYG8DC0GP1QKYAT
The core reading comprises the complete main texts and introductions of the first six books, the selected DN 2, 15, 16, 22, and 31, and the complete communal-harmony anthology. The Suttanipāta’s additional commentary can deepen a question without being counted as a different discourse collection.

**Demonstrate understanding:** explain the truths and their tasks, the connected path, conditional arising and cessation, aggregates, sense bases, not-self, and liberation with located passages. Compare a teaching across two collections. Explain one serious interpretive difficulty and why your preferred account is stronger than an alternative.

Develop a sustained textual investigation rather than a sequence of slogans: pose a question, reconstruct the relevant arguments or instructions, examine an objection, and revise the account after criticism. Use ordinary notes or Records; no prescribed page count or special submission mechanism is needed.

Name the works and editions actually read, and distinguish complete reading from selected reading. This describes intellectual study and its evidence; it does not certify meditative attainment. Proceed to [further readings](../further-reading/Further-reading.lingua#r_6G06H03HC5H1108GDYNEGQAEQS) or deepen a question through [textual study](Textual-study.lingua#r_5ATB59ZTXZHDEKYE94B3ZR5H74).

**Return:** [Buddhism: from the path to sustained inquiry](../Buddhism.lingua#r_7P61Z6HHZNZZ8ND3DHXSFSNRRE).
